  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Choose one next planning step using the local decision below. Return exactly one listed statement and stop. No other planning choices have been supplied.\n{\"context\": \"Each game has an approximate duration and a latest stopping time; progress reminders inform players without changing the rules.\", \"decision\": \"How should tables monitor their progress through a game?\", \"goal\": \"Help guests find compatible games, understand how to play, and enjoy the evening without avoidable waiting or confusion.\", \"problem\": \"A community room is hosting an evening of board games for adults with different levels of experience. Several tables, a host, willing game teachers, short waiting activities, and a varied collection are available. Guests can decline any game and join a later one. Every game has an agreed latest stopping time before the room closes, and ordinary refreshments and conversation have their own small areas.\"}\nCANDIDATE STATEMENTS:\nEach table sets a visible timer for agreed progress checkpoints and briefly reviews its remaining time when it rings. A shared signal prompts discussion without making one player personally responsible for watching the clock throughout play.\nThe host visits tables at agreed intervals and asks how progress compares with each game’s approximate duration. Tables receive a regular external reminder, and the host gains current information for guests awaiting their next game.\nA player at each table compares elapsed time with the game’s estimate and gives occasional progress reminders. Timing information comes from someone who understands the current position and can choose an unobtrusive moment to speak."
    }
  ],
